WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.773
Base rates: why “a study found” is not the end.

2
00:00:03.053 --> 00:00:13.827
In 2005, John Ioannidis argued in PLoS Medicine that most published research findings in many fields are false, and gave a simple model for why.

3
00:00:14.107 --> 00:00:31.515
The chance that a reported positive finding is true depends on three things: the prior odds that a tested hypothesis is true in that field, the study’s power to detect a real effect, and bias (selective reporting, flexible analysis, many variants tried with one reported).

4
00:00:31.795 --> 00:00:43.891
With a prior of 1 in 10, 80% power, and the conventional 5% false-positive rate, a positive finding is true only about 64% of the time, before any bias.

5
00:00:44.171 --> 00:00:46.880
AI is a field with all three risk factors.

6
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:51.981
Many claimed improvements are small, so power is low unless the eval is large.

7
00:00:52.261 --> 00:01:05.595
Researchers and vendors try many prompts, scaffolds, and settings and report the best, which is bias in Ioannidis’s sense; “The Leaderboard Illusion” (lesson 2) documented exactly this for arena submissions.

8
00:01:05.875 --> 00:01:15.432
And benchmarks leak into training, which is a form of leakage that Kapoor and Narayanan documented across hundreds of ML papers in 2022.

9
00:01:15.712 --> 00:01:24.245
The practical consequence: discount single, unreplicated results, especially surprising ones and especially ones with a small effect.

10
00:01:24.525 --> 00:01:27.789
Then ask what independent work, or your own eval, would show.
